<UserControl xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
             xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
             xmlns:controls="clr-namespace:KinesisEdit.Controls"
             xmlns:vm="clr-namespace:KinesisEdit.ViewModels"
             mc:Ignorable="d" d:DesignWidth="268" d:DesignHeight="600"
             x:Class="KinesisEdit.Views.KeyInspectorView"
             x:DataType="vm:KeyInspectorViewModel">
  <!--
    The key inspector rail — the Layout tab's third column (mockups 1e dark / 2a light).

    IT IS NOT MODAL. There is no scrim, no overlay host and no `HasActiveOverlay` anywhere near
    this file: the board beside it stays clickable while it is open, and clicking another cap
    simply refreshes the rail. That is the whole reason it replaces the feature panels it replaces.

    MONO IS THE FILE'S VOICE. The position token and the whole assignment line are mono, because
    `[d]` and `[esc]` are literally what the layout file contains; everything the app says in its
    own voice — "position", "factory", "now", the exclusivity sentence, the warning — is sans.
    Mockup 1e draws the header line entirely in sans; issue #92 tightens the token to mono, and
    that is the one deliberate departure from the mock's own typography here.

    THE RAIL NEVER COLLAPSES (issue #119). It used to hide its own frame when nothing was selected,
    so the parent's Auto column measured zero and the board jumped sideways to re-centre. It is a
    permanent column now: with no selection the frame stays exactly where it is and draws one muted
    sentence in place of the header, the modes and the footer. There is no close button either —
    Escape's last stage clears the selection, which lands in that same empty state.

    THE WIDTH IS NOT THIS FILE'S. It lives on the parent grid's third column
    (KeyboardEditorView.axaml), because a GridSplitter resizes columns and a local Width here would
    outrank whatever the column said. `IsWide` still travels — the editor folds it into the 300px
    macro floor — but nothing in this file reads it.
  -->
  <Border Classes="inspectorRail">
    <Panel>
      <!-- Nothing is selected: one line, muted, centred in the whole frame. It replaces the three
           rows below rather than joining them, so an empty rail is empty rather than a header with
           no content under it. -->
      <TextBlock Text="{x:Static vm:KeyInspectorViewModel.NoSelectionMessage}"
                 Classes="body muted"
                 TextWrapping="Wrap"
                 TextAlignment="Center"
                 Margin="{DynamicResource PaddingInspectorSection}"
                 HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                 VerticalAlignment="Center"
                 IsVisible="{Binding !HasSelection}" />

      <Grid RowDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto" IsVisible="{Binding HasSelection}">

        <!-- ===== The header ====================================================================
             The accent tile carrying the cap's own legend, then the position named in mono and the
             assignment line under it. The token is the FACTORY assignment on purpose: it is what
             identifies the position on the board however often it is remapped, which is exactly why
             the line below it can then say what the position does now. -->
        <Border Grid.Row="0" Classes="inspectorSection">
          <!-- TWO COLUMNS, not three. The third used to carry a ghost close button; the rail cannot
               be closed any more (issue #119), so there is nothing for it to hold. -->
          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*" ColumnSpacing="10">
            <Border Grid.Column="0" Classes="inspectorKeyTile" VerticalAlignment="Center">
              <!-- TextPrimary, NOT AccentText. `AccentText` is the colour that sits on a solid
                   accent *fill* — white on the light variant — and this tile is a 14% tint over a
                   light surface, where white is invisible. The mock's own value here is the
                   near-white it calls body text, which is what TextPrimary is in both variants. -->
              <TextBlock Text="{Binding CapCaption}"
                         Classes="monoValue"
                         Foreground="{DynamicResource TextPrimaryBrush}"
                         TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                         HorizontalAlignment="Center"
                         VerticalAlignment="Center" />
            </Border>

            <StackPanel Grid.Column="1" Spacing="2" VerticalAlignment="Center">
              <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="5">
                <TextBlock Text="{Binding PositionDescription}"
                           Classes="cardTitle"
                           IsVisible="{Binding HasPositionDescription}"
                           VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                <TextBlock Text="{x:Static vm:KeyInspectorViewModel.Separator}"
                           Classes="cardTitle muted"
                           IsVisible="{Binding HasPositionDescription}"
                           VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                <TextBlock Text="{Binding PositionToken}"
                           Classes="monoValue"
                           TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
                           VerticalAlignment="Center" />
                <TextBlock Text="{x:Static vm:KeyInspectorViewModel.PositionSuffix}"
                           Classes="cardTitle"
                           VerticalAlignment="Center" />
              </StackPanel>

              <!-- `factory [d] · now [esc]`, mono end to end: every part of it is either a value in
                   the layout file or the punctuation between two of them. -->
              <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="4">
                <TextBlock Text="{x:Static vm:KeyInspectorViewModel.FactoryLabel}" Classes="monoValueSmall muted" />
                <TextBlock Text="{Binding FactoryAssignmentText}" Classes="monoValueSmall muted" />
                <TextBlock Text="{x:Static vm:KeyInspectorViewModel.Separator}" Classes="monoValueSmall muted" />
                <TextBlock Text="{x:Static vm:KeyInspectorViewModel.CurrentLabel}" Classes="monoValueSmall muted" />
                <TextBlock Text="{Binding CurrentAssignmentText}"
                           Classes="monoValueSmall"
                           TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis" />
              </StackPanel>
            </StackPanel>

          </Grid>
        </Border>

        <!-- ===== The body ======================================================================
             Either the mode machinery or, for a position that can never be remapped, the locked-key
             panel in place of all of it. Scrolled, because a panel is free to be taller than 268
             px of rail. -->
        <ScrollViewer Grid.Row="1" HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled">
          <Panel>
            <!-- Resolved by ViewLocator, like every other view model the app hosts by content:
                 a DataContext-swapping child would have to reach back up the visual tree for the
                 `IsLocked` it is switched by, and a view gets its data from its own DataContext. -->
            <ContentControl Content="{Binding LockedPanel}" IsVisible="{Binding IsLocked}" />

            <StackPanel IsVisible="{Binding !IsLocked}">

              <!-- ===== The modes ==============================================================
                   A 2x2 grid on the section strip's own TabStrip / TabStripItem control themes
                   (docs/design/handoff.md § "Key inspector panels"). Two rows, so the strip is
                   twice the theme's own one-row height; the grid itself is the ItemsPanel.

                   `Multi-mod` is not in this list on a board whose firmware lacks it — the tab is
                   absent, not dim (KeyInspectorViewModel builds the set). -->
              <Border Classes="inspectorSection" Padding="0">
                <StackPanel>
                  <TabStrip Theme="{StaticResource TabStrip}"
                            Height="76"
                            ItemsSource="{Binding Tabs}"
                            SelectedValueBinding="{Binding Mode, DataType=vm:KeyInspectorTabViewModel}"
                            SelectedValue="{Binding SelectedMode, Mode=OneWay}"
                            SelectionChanged="OnModeSelectionChanged">
                    <TabStrip.Styles>
                      <Style Selector="TabStripItem">
                        <Setter Property="IsEnabled" Value="{Binding IsEnabled, DataType=vm:KeyInspectorTabViewModel}" />
                      </Style>
                    </TabStrip.Styles>
                    <TabStrip.ItemsPanel>
                      <ItemsPanelTemplate>
                        <UniformGrid Columns="2" />
                      </ItemsPanelTemplate>
                    </TabStrip.ItemsPanel>
                    <TabStrip.ItemTemplate>
                      <DataTemplate DataType="vm:KeyInspectorTabViewModel">
                        <TextBlock Text="{Binding DisplayCaption}" TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis" />
                      </DataTemplate>
                    </TabStrip.ItemTemplate>
                  </TabStrip>

                  <StackPanel Margin="{DynamicResource PaddingInspectorSection}" Spacing="8">
                    <!-- 2a's tightened wording, which supersedes 1e's shorter "This key does one
                         thing". The app in its own voice, so sans.

                         IT IS A HINT BLOCK AND DELIBERATELY NOT AMBER (issue #119). As a bare line
                         of prose it read as stray text between the tabs and the warning below it;
                         framed, the two stop reading as one block. Neutral, because amber is
                         reserved for advisories in the four-status vocabulary and this sentence
                         warns of nothing — it states how the one slot works. The `i` mark is the
                         icon system's own IconInformation, at the icon law's 16px. -->
                    <Border Classes="hintBlock">
                      <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*" ColumnSpacing="8">
                        <controls:Icon Grid.Column="0"
                                       Data="{DynamicResource IconInformation}"
                                       Stroke="{DynamicResource TextMutedBrush}"
                                       VerticalAlignment="Top" />
                        <TextBlock Grid.Column="1"
                                   Text="{x:Static vm:KeyInspectorViewModel.ExclusivitySentence}"
                                   Classes="body muted"
                                   TextWrapping="Wrap" />
                      </Grid>
                    </Border>

                    <!-- Stated at the point of switching rather than after (mockup 2h). Amber and
                         non-blocking: it says what the assignment will replace, it does not ask. -->
                    <Border Background="{DynamicResource StatusAdvisoryTintBrush}"
                            BorderBrush="{DynamicResource StatusAdvisoryTintBorderBrush}"
                            BorderThickness="1"
                            CornerRadius="{DynamicResource RadiusControl}"
                            Padding="9,7"
                            IsVisible="{Binding HasModeSwitchWarning}">
                      <TextBlock Text="{Binding ModeSwitchWarning}"
                                 Classes="body statusWarning"
                                 TextWrapping="Wrap" />
                    </Border>
                  </StackPanel>
                </StackPanel>
              </Border>

              <!-- The showing mode's own panel, resolved by ViewLocator from whichever
                   KeyInspectorPanelViewModel the editor handed the rail. Empty for a mode with no
                   panel — which since issue #93 is only Multi-mod, on the boards that have it. -->
              <ContentControl Content="{Binding ActivePanel}" />
            </StackPanel>
          </Panel>
        </ScrollViewer>

        <!-- ===== The footer ====================================================================
             Always present (docs/design/handoff.md § "Layout tab"): the key's advisory note, then
             the two actions. `Copy to…` is the EDITOR's CopyKeyCommand, the arm-then-click-a-target
             pick issue #91 built. `Revert key` is the rail's RevertKeyCommand, which offers the
             showing panel first refusal (TryRevert — the Macro panel's whole state lives in
             Macro1..Macro5, which a remap reset does not touch) and otherwise runs the editor's own
             ResetKeyCommand, which calls ClearRemap() and deliberately not Remap(OriginalKey).
             Nothing is reimplemented here.

             Hidden for a locked position: the locked panel carries its own copy actions and there
             is no remap to revert. -->
        <Border Grid.Row="2" Classes="inspectorFooter" IsVisible="{Binding !IsLocked}">
          <StackPanel Spacing="8">
            <!-- The note is READ off the editor's advisory set, never rescanned. Amber, never red,
                 and it blocks nothing. -->
            <!-- A Grid, not a horizontal StackPanel: a StackPanel measures its children with
                 infinite width, so TextWrapping would never fire and a long note would run off the
                 268px rail. -->
            <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*"
                  ColumnSpacing="6"
                  IsVisible="{Binding HasAdvisory}">
              <Ellipse Grid.Column="0"
                       Width="6"
                       Height="6"
                       Fill="{DynamicResource StatusAdvisoryStrongBrush}"
                       VerticalAlignment="Top"
                       Margin="0,4,0,0" />
              <TextBlock Grid.Column="1"
                         Text="{Binding AdvisoryNote}"
                         Classes="body statusWarning"
                         TextWrapping="Wrap" />
            </Grid>

            <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="8">
              <Button Classes="secondary"
                      Content="{x:Static vm:KeyInspectorViewModel.RevertKeyCaption}"
                      Command="{Binding RevertKeyCommand}" />
              <Button Classes="secondary"
                      Content="{x:Static vm:KeyInspectorViewModel.CopyToCaption}"
                      Command="{Binding CopyKeyCommand}"
                      IsVisible="{Binding !IsCopyArmed}" />
              <!-- The same slot, once the pick is armed: the button that started it is what ends
                   it, so the rail never grows a dead control it only sometimes needs. -->
              <Button Classes="secondary"
                      Content="{x:Static vm:KeyInspectorViewModel.CancelCopyCaption}"
                      Command="{Binding CancelCopyKeyCommand}"
                      IsVisible="{Binding IsCopyArmed}" />
            </StackPanel>
          </StackPanel>
        </Border>
      </Grid>
    </Panel>
  </Border>
</UserControl>
